            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Conquest of Gaul</strong><br>\nFrom 58 BC to 50 BC, Caesar led a campaign to conquer Gaul (modern France and Belgium), which expanded Roman territory to the Atlantic Ocean. His success in Gaul made him immensely popular and wealthy, which threatened the power of the Senate and Pompey, who had once been an ally but had now turned against him.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Crossing the Rubicon</strong><br>\nIn 49 BC, fearing Caesar's power, the Senate ordered him to disband his army and return to Rome. Instead, Caesar famously crossed the Rubicon River with his army, igniting a civil war. \"Crossing the Rubicon\" is now a phrase that means passing a point of no return. This bold move led to a three-year war for control of Rome.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Dictatorship</strong><br>\nAfter a series of civil wars, Caesar emerged as the victor and was appointed dictator for life. As dictator, he implemented many reforms, including calendar reform, restructuring debt, and increasing the Senate's size. His rule became increasingly autocratic over time, sparking concern among those who feared the end of the Republic.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
