          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Ides of March</strong><br>\nDissent among the senators about Caesar's power led to his assassination on March 15, 44 BC, known as the Ides of March. He was stabbed to death in the Senate by a group of senators, including his friend Brutus. Caesar's death was a pivotal event that led to the end of the Roman Republic and the rise of the Roman Empire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nCaesar's legacy includes his military genius, his contribution to the reorganization of the Roman government, and the Julian calendar, which is the basis of the calendar we use today. He also left behind a vast amount of writings, providing insight into his military strategies and political policies.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Language and Culture</strong><br>\nCaesar's influence extends beyond politics and military; the title \"Caesar\" became a synonym for \"Emperor,\" influencing various languages and the concept of leadership. His adopted heir, Augustus, became the first emperor of Rome, marking the transition from Republic to Empire and solidifying Caesar's lasting impact on world history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
